# Single-Call Reasoning

## Overview

A customer asks whether an order delivered 40 days ago can still be refunded.
The answer needs a few reasoning steps but no tools. Chain of Thought, Chain of
Draft, and Algorithm of Thoughts each answer it with one structured model call
and return the parsed reasoning next to the answer:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{reply.data.output, reply.data.reasoning.steps_count}
# => {"No. The 30-day refund window closed 10 days ago.", 3}
```

You'll run all three Flows offline, read the prompt and output format each one
sends, and compare their result data. Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.0"}])
```

## Three Agents

Each Agent selects one Flow. None declares tools; these Flows never send tool
definitions to the model.

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.ChainAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent, id: "my_app/chain_agent", vsn: 1, name: "chain_agent"

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ChainOfThought
  end
end

defmodule MyApp.DraftAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent, id: "my_app/draft_agent", vsn: 1, name: "draft_agent"

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ChainOfDraft
  end
end

defmodule MyApp.AlgorithmAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent, id: "my_app/algorithm_agent", vsn: 1, name: "algorithm_agent"

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.AlgorithmOfThoughts
  end
end
```

## The Shared Graph

The three Flows have the same shape. Print one root Flow and its model round:

```elixir
alias Jido.AI.Reasoning.ChainOfThought

for flow <- [ChainOfThought, ChainOfThought.ModelRound] do
  IO.puts("Graph: #{inspect(flow)}")

  flow.flow().components
  |> Enum.sort_by(&elem(&1, 0))
  |> Enum.each(fn {name, component} -> IO.puts("  #{name}: #{component.kind}") end)
end
```

```text
Graph: Jido.AI.Reasoning.ChainOfThought
  finalize: call
  input_guard: call
  reason: invoke
  start_request: call
Graph: Jido.AI.Reasoning.ChainOfThought.ModelRound
  complete: call
  model: call
  output_guard: call
  project: call
  record_attempt: call
```

The root Flow is the ReAct root with a fixed `output_schema`, one model call,
and no retries. `ModelRound` has no loop: it projects the conversation, calls
the model once with tools off, guards the output, records the attempt, and
`complete` parses the structured object into the result.

## Offline Model

The fake provider returns a JSON object as the model's text. ReqLLM decodes it
into the response object and validates it against the Flow's schema:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.LLM do
  def options do
    %{
      api_key: "offline-test-key",
      max_retries: 0,
      max_tokens: 512,
      temperature: 0.0,
      req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, __MODULE__}]
    }
  end

  def object(conn, object), do: text(conn, Jason.encode!(object))

  def text(conn, text) do
    Req.Test.json(conn, %{
      "id" => "resp_#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}",
      "object" => "response",
      "created_at" => 1,
      "model" => "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18",
      "status" => "completed",
      "output" => [
        %{
          "type" => "message",
          "id" => "msg_1",
          "role" => "assistant",
          "status" => "completed",
          "content" => [%{"type" => "output_text", "text" => text, "annotations" => []}]
        }
      ],
      "usage" => %{
        "input_tokens" => 60,
        "input_tokens_details" => %{"cached_tokens" => 0},
        "output_tokens" => 40,
        "output_tokens_details" => %{"reasoning_tokens" => 0},
        "total_tokens" => 100
      }
    })
  end

  def body(conn) do
    {:ok, body, _conn} = Plug.Conn.read_body(conn)
    Jason.decode!(body)
  end
end
```

`ask` runs one request on a fresh Agent. It reports each provider request body
to the notebook process:

```elixir
notebook = self()
question = "Order A-1001 was delivered 40 days ago. Can the customer still get a refund?"

ask = fn agent_module, object ->
  Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
    send(notebook, {:request, MyApp.LLM.body(conn)})
    MyApp.LLM.object(conn, object)
  end)

  agent = agent_module.instantiate!(id: "agent-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}")
  signal = agent_module.request_signal!(%{input: question, options: MyApp.LLM.options()})

  {:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} = agent_module.cmd(agent, signal)

  request =
    receive do
      {:request, body} -> body
    after
      1_000 -> nil
    end

  {reply, request}
end
```

## Chain of Thought

Chain of Thought asks for numbered steps and a conclusion:

```elixir
{reply, request} =
  ask.(MyApp.ChainAgent, %{
    steps: [
      %{number: 1, content: "The refund window is 30 days from delivery."},
      %{number: 2, content: "The order was delivered 40 days ago."},
      %{number: 3, content: "40 is greater than 30, so the window has closed."}
    ],
    conclusion: "No. The 30-day refund window closed 10 days ago."
  })

{reply.data.status, reply.data.output, reply.data.model_calls}
```

The result is
`{:completed, "No. The 30-day refund window closed 10 days ago.", 1}`.
`output` is the conclusion. The parsed chain is in `reasoning`:

```elixir
reply.data.reasoning
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  method: :chain_of_thought,
  steps: [
    %{number: 1, content: "The refund window is 30 days from delivery."},
    %{number: 2, content: "The order was delivered 40 days ago."},
    %{number: 3, content: "40 is greater than 30, so the window has closed."}
  ],
  steps_count: 3,
  conclusion: "No. The 30-day refund window closed 10 days ago."
}
```

`reply.data.reasoning_steps` copies `steps_count`, so it's `3`.

The request carried the default instructions as a system message and the
output schema as a strict JSON schema:

```elixir
[system | _messages] = request["input"]

{hd(system["content"])["text"], request["text"]["format"]["type"],
 request["text"]["format"]["schema"]["required"], Map.has_key?(request, "tools")}
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{"You are a helpful AI assistant that thinks through problems step by step.\nReturn an ordered list of clear reasoning steps and one concise conclusion.\n",
 "json_schema", ["conclusion", "steps"], false}
```

## Chain of Draft

Chain of Draft asks for drafts of at most five words each and a final answer:

```elixir
{reply, request} =
  ask.(MyApp.DraftAgent, %{
    drafts: ["window: 30 days", "delivered: 40 days", "40 > 30: closed"],
    answer: "No. The refund window has closed."
  })

{reply.data.output, reply.data.reasoning}
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{"No. The refund window has closed.",
 %{
   method: :chain_of_draft,
   drafts: ["window: 30 days", "delivered: 40 days", "40 > 30: closed"],
   steps_count: 3,
   conclusion: "No. The refund window has closed."
 }}
```

```elixir
request["text"]["format"]["schema"]["required"]
```

The result is `["answer", "drafts"]`. The five-word limit lives in the
instructions only; the schema accepts any non-empty string.

## Algorithm of Thoughts

Algorithm of Thoughts asks for the operation sequence of an explicit search,
including backtracking, and a final answer:

```elixir
{reply, request} =
  ask.(MyApp.AlgorithmAgent, %{
    operations: [
      "Check the standard 30-day window: 40 days fails",
      "Check the damaged-item exception: no damage reported, backtrack",
      "Check store credit within 60 days: 40 days passes"
    ],
    answer: "No cash refund. Offer store credit, which is allowed up to 60 days."
  })

{reply.data.output, reply.data.reasoning.method, reply.data.reasoning.steps_count}
```

The result is
`{"No cash refund. Offer store credit, which is allowed up to 60 days.", :algorithm_of_thoughts, 3}`.
The operations are in `reply.data.reasoning.operations`.

```elixir
request["text"]["format"]["schema"]["required"]
```

The result is `["answer", "operations"]`.

## Compare the Flows

| Flow | Default instructions ask for | Required JSON | `reasoning` fields | Default `options` |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ChainOfThought` | Ordered steps and one concise conclusion | `steps` (`number`, `content`), `conclusion` | `steps`, `steps_count`, `conclusion` | `max_tokens: 512, receive_timeout: 30_000, temperature: 0.0` |
| `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ChainOfDraft` | Steps of at most five words and one final answer | `drafts` (strings), `answer` | `drafts`, `steps_count`, `conclusion` | `max_tokens: 256, temperature: 0.0` |
| `Jido.AI.Reasoning.AlgorithmOfThoughts` | An algorithmic search with backtracking, the operation sequence, and the answer | `operations` (strings), `answer` | `operations`, `steps_count`, `conclusion` | `max_tokens: 1024, temperature: 0.0` |

Every `reasoning` map also has `method`. Each list needs at least one item.
Read the instructions with `default_instructions/0` on each module, and
replace them with the `instructions` input. Your instructions replace the
format request too, so keep asking for the same fields.

Choose by the answer you need:

- **Chain of Thought** when a reviewer or the customer needs to read each
  step, such as explaining a refund decision.
- **Chain of Draft** when the reasoning is short and you want fewer output
  tokens, such as checking a date against one policy window.
- **Algorithm of Thoughts** when the model must try options in order and
  discard dead ends, such as walking through refund, exception, and store
  credit rules.
- **ReAct** when the answer needs live data. These Flows can't call
  `MyApp.LookupOrder`.

## When the Format Fails

There is one model call and no repair round. ReqLLM validates the response
against the schema, so plain text fails the call and ends the request:

```elixir
Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
  MyApp.LLM.text(conn, "No, the refund window has closed.")
end)

agent = MyApp.ChainAgent.instantiate!(id: "chain-plain-text")
signal = MyApp.ChainAgent.request_signal!(%{input: question, options: MyApp.LLM.options()})
{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} = MyApp.ChainAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

Map.take(reply.data, [:status, :reason_code, :reason, :output, :reasoning])
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  status: :failed,
  reason_code: :model,
  reason:
    "Structured output failed final validation: No complete structured output value was materialized.",
  output: "",
  reasoning: %{method: :chain_of_thought, steps: [], steps_count: 0, conclusion: nil}
}
```

| Cause | `status` | `reason_code` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Provider error, or text that isn't the required object | `:failed` | `:model` |
| Output guard rejected the answer | `:blocked` | `:output` |
| A budget refused the call | `:blocked` | `:limits` |

Failed and blocked results keep the `reasoning` keys with empty values.

## Inputs

All three Flows accept the same request fields: `input`, `instructions`,
`model`, `options`, `prompt_cache`, `context` (`max_messages`, `max_bytes`),
and `limits` (`elapsed_ms`, `input_tokens`, `output_tokens`, `total_tokens`,
`total_cost`). Unknown keys fail validation, including the ReAct-only
`fallback_models`, `tool_policy`, and `limits.model_calls`:

```elixir
{:error, error} =
  Jido.AI.Reasoning.ChainOfDraft.validate_params(%{input: question, fallback_models: []})

error.__struct__
```

The result is `Jido.Action.Error.InvalidInputError`.

## Summary

- Chain of Thought, Chain of Draft, and Algorithm of Thoughts make exactly
  one model call with no tools, retries, or fallbacks.
- Each sends a strict JSON schema and parses the object into
  `reply.data.reasoning`.
- `output` is the conclusion or answer; `reasoning.steps_count` counts steps,
  drafts, or operations, and `reasoning_steps` copies it.
- A response without the object ends the request as `:failed` or
  `:blocked`, with empty `reasoning` values.

